# Vulnerabilities accepted for the release image scan, with an expiry review note each.
# Everything else — anything in the application's own dependency tree, and every OS package —
# still blocks promotion (see the image-scan job in .github/workflows/release.yml).

# CVE-2026-102276 and CVE-2026-102278 (GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p, GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7): brace-expansion
# denial of service through uncontrolled recursion, fixed upstream in 5.0.10 and 5.0.11.
#
# The only copy left in the image is the one bundled inside the npm CLI
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion, 5.0.9 in npm 12.1.0, the newest
# release and the one the Dockerfile installs). npm is never on the request path: the entrypoint
# runs `node dist/main`, and npm is present only for the documented operator runbooks
# (`docker compose run --rm openwa-api npm run migration:run:prod`). Reaching this code requires an
# operator to run an npm command with an attacker-chosen glob inside the container.
#
# These are ID-level ignores, so they would also silence the same CVEs in the application tree,
# which this file otherwise refuses to do. What keeps that honest is the root `overrides` entries
# that pin brace-expansion per minimatch major (^1.1.21 under minimatch 3, ^2.1.7 under minimatch 5
# and 9, ^5.0.12 under minimatch 10): npm cannot resolve the application tree below the fixed
# versions, so there is no app-tree finding for these ids to hide. Raise those floors alongside any
# future brace-expansion advisory rather than widening this list. Drop both entries once npm ships a
# bundle with brace-expansion >= 5.0.11.
CVE-2026-102276
CVE-2026-102278

# CVE-2026-19534 (GHSA-rfgv-xxqx-mfg5): undici denial of service, fixed upstream in 6.28.1, 7.29.1
# and 8.10.2.
#
# Same shape as the entries above: the only affected copy is the one bundled inside the npm CLI
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/undici, 6.28.0 in npm 12.1.0). The application tree
# is at the fix on its own: undici is a direct dependency at ^8.10.2, which package-lock.json
# resolves to 8.10.2, so this ID-level ignore cannot hide an application-tree finding. Drop this
# entry once npm ships a bundle with undici >= 6.28.1.
CVE-2026-19534

# Chromium: no accepted advisory, and the two architectures are not scanned alike.
#
# arm64 installs Debian's `chromium` and `chromium-sandbox` (Dockerfile, TARGETARCH branch) by choice;
# Chrome for Testing publishes linux-arm64 builds only from 153 on, and the image has not moved arm64 to
# one. Those are dpkg packages, so the scan sees them and a fixable HIGH/CRITICAL fails it.
# `ignore-unfixed` still hides every chromium CVE Debian has not fixed yet, so a passing arm64 scan
# does not mean the browser has no open advisories. Measured on 2026-09-15 against
# ghcr.io/rmyndharis/openwa:main (arm64, the release flags minus `ignore-unfixed`): `chromium`,
# `chromium-common` and `chromium-sandbox` each carry 2 CRITICAL and 15 HIGH advisories in state
# "affected", i.e. Debian has published no fix. That exposure is accepted for the release: the
# alternative is moving arm64 off the Debian package to Chrome for Testing, which publishes
# linux-arm64 builds from 153 on. Re-measure with
# `trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --platform linux/arm64 ghcr.io/rmyndharis/openwa:<tag>`
# when deciding whether the number still fits. The eleven chromium CVEs this file used to accept
# (between CVE-2026-76033 and CVE-2026-76047) were fixed in 151.0.7922.169, and bookworm-security has
# shipped newer chromium builds since.
#
# amd64 downloads a Chrome for Testing binary into /opt/puppeteer. No dpkg package owns it and no
# lockfile names it, so neither the OS nor the library scanner can see it: a clean amd64 scan says
# nothing about the browser. Its version is the pin in the Dockerfile, and no scan will flag that pin
# as outdated.

# CVE-2026-95282, CVE-2026-95293, CVE-2026-95301, CVE-2026-95304, CVE-2026-95306, CVE-2026-95315,
# CVE-2026-95324, CVE-2026-95333, CVE-2026-95347, CVE-2026-95351, CVE-2026-95354, CVE-2026-95355,
# CVE-2026-95373 and CVE-2026-95380: Chromium advisories fixed upstream in 154.0.8037.57. Every one is
# published as affecting Chrome "prior to 154.0.8037.57".
#
# These accept nothing the image is exposed to. arm64 installs Debian's bookworm rebuild of that same
# release, 154.0.8037.57-1~deb12u1, so it carries the fixes. Debian records the fix only against the
# unstable build 154.0.8037.57-1 (DSA-6513-1 covers trixie), and `~deb12u1` sorts below `-1`, so the
# scanner reports the bookworm rebuild as unfixed.
#
# Drop all fourteen once bookworm-security publishes the 154.0.8037.92-1~deb12u1 binaries the Debian
# tracker already lists as its version (checked 2026-10-01, when the archive still served
# 154.0.8037.57-1~deb12u1). The next image installs it, and it sorts above the recorded fix.
CVE-2026-95282
CVE-2026-95293
CVE-2026-95301
CVE-2026-95304
CVE-2026-95306
CVE-2026-95315
CVE-2026-95324
CVE-2026-95333
CVE-2026-95347
CVE-2026-95351
CVE-2026-95354
CVE-2026-95355
CVE-2026-95373
CVE-2026-95380

# CVE-2024-28757, CVE-2025-59375, CVE-2026-25210, CVE-2026-45186, CVE-2026-66046 and CVE-2026-93990:
# libexpat1, fixed in Debian's 2.5.0-1+deb12u4.
#
# amd64 only, and temporary. bookworm-security publishes 2.5.0-1+deb12u4 for arm64, which the
# Dockerfile's `apt-get upgrade` installs, but on 2026-10-01 it still served 2.5.0-1+deb12u3 for
# amd64, so the amd64 image cannot install the fix yet. Each CVE needs attacker-supplied XML parsed by
# expat. In the image the browser links it, but Blink parses web content's XML with the libxml2 the
# browser bundles; fontconfig, Mesa and D-Bus use it on their local configuration files, and Node.js
# does not link it.
#
# Drop all six once bookworm-security serves 2.5.0-1+deb12u4 for amd64.
CVE-2024-28757
CVE-2025-59375
CVE-2026-25210
CVE-2026-45186
CVE-2026-66046
CVE-2026-93990
